    "knowledge_point": "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions",
    "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-2 & >3-2 \\\\ x-2 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x>\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x>3.$",
